{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1816 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "e38edc8e6d4603188d205483f9d8399cec13cf69653b4b2ba9416d442b031ba3" :edb/db-before-t 1815 :edb/db-after-t 1816 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535128 50 #inst "2026-10-08T17:33:54.345Z" 13194139535128 true] [17592186090167 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332625 13194139535128 true] [17592186090167 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332625 13194139535128 true] [17592186090168 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332623 13194139535128 true] [17592186090168 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332624 13194139535128 true] [17592186090168 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332624 13194139535128 true] [17592186090170 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332626 13194139535128 true] [17592186090170 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332627 13194139535128 true] [17592186090170 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332627 13194139535128 true] [17592186091884 1100 "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)=5^{2x}-20$ in the interval $[0,2]$. Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point $x_0 = 1$. Round your answer to three decimal places." 13194139535128 false] [17592186091884 1100 "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)=5^{2x}-20$ in the interval $[0,2].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point $x_0 = 1.$ Round your answer to three decimal places." 13194139535128 true] [17592186091884 1145 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n$.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x) = (5^{2x}-20)' = 2\\ln (5)\\cdot 5^{2x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{n + 1} &= x_{n} - (5^{2x_{n}} - 20)/(2\\ln (5) \\cdot 5^{2x_{n}})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0 = 1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= x_{0} - (5^{2x_{0}} - 20)/(2\\ln (5) \\cdot 5^{2x_{0}}). \\\\\n&= 1 - (5^{2} - 20)/(2\\ln (5) \\cdot 5^{2}). \\\\\n&\\approx 0.938\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535128 false] [17592186091884 1145 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x) = (5^{2x}-20)' = 2\\ln (5)\\cdot 5^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{5}^{2{x}_{n}}-20}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)\\cdot {5}^{2{x}_{n}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0 = 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{5}^{2{x}_{0}}-20}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)\\cdot {5}^{2{x}_{0}}}. \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{5}^{2}-20}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)\\cdot {5}^{2}}. \\\\  & ≈0.938,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535128 true] [17592186091885 1100 "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)=e^x-x^3-3$ in the interval $[4,6]$. Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point $x_0 = 5$. Round your answer to three decimal places." 13194139535128 false] [17592186091885 1100 "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)=e^x-x^3-3$ in the interval $[4,6].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point $x_0 = 5.$ Round your answer to three decimal places." 13194139535128 true] [17592186091885 1145 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n$.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x) = (e^x-x^3-3)' = e^x-3x^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{n + 1} &= x_{n} - \\frac{e^{x_{n}} - x_{n}^{3} - 3}{e^{x_{n}} - 3x_{n}^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0 = 5$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= x_{0} - \\frac{e^{x_{0}} - x_{0}^{3} - 3}{e^{x_{0}} - 3x_{0}^{2}} \\\\\n&= 5 - (e^{5} - 5^{3} - 3)/(e^{5} - 3(5)^{2}) \\\\\n&= 4.721941…\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= x_{1} - \\frac{e^{x_{1}} - x_{1}^{3} - 3}{e^{x_{1}} - 3x_{1}^{2}} \\\\\n&= (4.721941…) - (e^{4.721941…} - (4.721941…)^{3} - 3)/(e^{4.721941…} - 3(4.721941…)^{2}) \\\\\n&\\approx 4.632\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535128 false] [17592186091885 1145 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x) = (e^x-x^3-3)' = e^x-3x^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-{x}_{n}^{3}-3}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-3{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0 = 5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-{x}_{0}^{3}-3}{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-3{x}_{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =5-\\frac{{e}^{5}-{5}^{3}-3}{{e}^{5}-3(5)^{2}} \\\\  & =4.721\\,941…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-{x}_{1}^{3}-3}{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-3{x}_{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =(4.721\\,941…)-\\frac{{e}^{4.721\\,941…}-(4.721\\,941…)^{3}-3}{{e}^{4.721\\,941…}-3(4.721\\,941…)^{2}} \\\\  & ≈4.632,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535128 true] [17592186091886 1100 "Given that the function $f(x)=x^5-2$ has a root in the interval $[0,2]$, use the Newton-Raphson process starting from $x_0=1$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to two decimal places." 13194139535128 false] [17592186091886 1100 "Given that the function $f(x)=x^5-2$ has a root in the interval $[0,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process starting from $x_0=1$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to two decimal places." 13194139535128 true] [17592186091886 1145 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n$.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x) = (x^5-2)' = 5x^4\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{n + 1} &= x_{n} - \\frac{x_{n}^{5} - 2}{5x_{n}^{4}} \\\\\n&= (x_{n}(5x_{n}^{4}) - (x_{n}^{5} - 2))/(5x_{n}^{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4x_{n}^{5} + 2}{5x_{n}^{4}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0 = 1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= \\frac{4x_{0}^{5} + 2}{5x_{0}^{4}} \\\\\n&= (4(1)^{5} + 2)/(5(1)^{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{5} \\\\\n&= 1.2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= \\frac{4x_{1}^{5} + 2}{5x_{1}^{4}} \\\\\n&= (4(1.2)^{5} + 2)/(5(1.2)^{4}) \\\\\n&= 1.152… \\\\\nx_{3} &= \\frac{4x_{2}^{5} + 2}{5x_{2}^{4}} \\\\\n&= (4(1.152…)^{5} + 2)/(5(1.152…)^{4}) \\\\\n&= 1.148…\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations\n\n$$\nx_2 \\approx 1.15\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx_3 \\approx 1.15\n$$\n\nare the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is\n\n$$\nx \\approx 1.15\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139535128 false] [17592186091886 1145 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x) = (x^5-2)' = 5x^4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{5}-2}{5{x}_{n}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(5{x}_{n}^{4})-({x}_{n}^{5}-2)}{5{x}_{n}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}_{n}^{5}+2}{5{x}_{n}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0 = 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{4{x}_{0}^{5}+2}{5{x}_{0}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1)^{5}+2}{5(1)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5} \\\\  & =1.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{4{x}_{1}^{5}+2}{5{x}_{1}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1.2)^{5}+2}{5(1.2)^{4}} \\\\  & =1.152… \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{4{x}_{2}^{5}+2}{5{x}_{2}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1.152…)^{5}+2}{5(1.152…)^{4}} \\\\  & =1.148….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations $x_2 \\approx 1.15$ and $x_3 \\approx 1.15$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x \\approx 1.15,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139535128 true] [17592186092317 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332628 13194139535128 true] [17592186092317 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332631 13194139535128 true] [17592186092317 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332634 13194139535128 true] [17592186092317 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332661 13194139535128 true] [17592186092318 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332637 13194139535128 true] [17592186092318 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332640 13194139535128 true] [17592186092318 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332643 13194139535128 true] [17592186092318 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332657 13194139535128 true] [17592186092319 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332648 13194139535128 true] [17592186092319 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332651 13194139535128 true] [17592186092319 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332654 13194139535128 true] [17592186092319 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332665 13194139535128 true] [17592186092319 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332669 13194139535128 true] [17592186092607 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535128 true] [17592186092607 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nSuppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x) = \\ln x - 1$ in the interval $[1, 3]$. Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point $x_{0} = 1.5$. Round your answers to three decimal places.$x_{1} \\approx$\n{{field-1}}\n$, x_{2} \\approx$\n{{field-2}}\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"finding-a-functions-root-using-the-newton-raphson-method\"></a>" 13194139535128 false] [17592186092607 1100 "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1$ in the interval $[1,3].$ Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=1.5.$ Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,{x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535128 true] [17592186092607 1145 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}-1}{(\\frac{1}{{x}_{n}})} \\\\  & ={x}_{n}-({x}_{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}-{x}_{n}) \\\\  & =2{x}_{n}-{x}_{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =2{x}_{0}-{x}_{0}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{0} \\\\  & =2(1.5)-(1.5)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5) \\\\  & =2.391\\,802….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2{x}_{1}-{x}_{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{1} \\\\  & =2(2.391\\,802…)-(2.391\\,802…)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2.391\\,802…) \\\\  & =2.697\\,840….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}≈2.392$ and ${x}_{2}≈2.698,$ rounded to three decimal places." 13194139535128 true] [17592186092609 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535128 true] [17592186092609 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nSuppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x) = x^{2} + x - 3$ in the interval $[0, 2]$. Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point $x_{0} = 0.8$.$x_{1} =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535128 false] [17592186092609 1100 "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+x-3$ in the interval $[0,2].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=0.8.$\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535128 true] [17592186092609 1145 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{2}+x-3)^{′}=2x+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{2}+{x}_{n}-3}{2{x}_{n}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(2{x}_{n}+1)-({x}_{n}^{2}+{x}_{n}-3)}{2{x}_{n}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}^{2}+3}{2{x}_{n}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{{x}_{0}^{2}+3}{2{x}_{0}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.8)^{2}+3}{2(0.8)+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3.64}{2.6} \\\\  & =1.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332623 1034 "1.500" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332623 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535128 true] [17592186332623 1111 #uuid "9d9b345d-b1d5-560a-9b78-65330febce09" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332624 1034 "2.392" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332624 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535128 true] [17592186332624 1111 #uuid "915cb2d1-2f8d-5ef9-bef0-487cc05f8611" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332625 1034 "2.698" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332625 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535128 true] [17592186332625 1111 #uuid "171f0d0e-90eb-5b9b-aed8-770e65332933" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332626 1034 "0.2" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332626 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535128 true] [17592186332626 1111 #uuid "a80648c5-d364-5076-91b6-74f27059f5ff" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332627 1034 "1.4" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332627 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535128 true] [17592186332627 1111 #uuid "925d7e4d-d537-50e5-ac6e-7917e5d90565" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332628 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332629 13194139535128 true] [17592186332628 1089 "q-256466" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332628 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535128 true] [17592186332628 1100 "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-2$ in the interval $[-2,0].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=-1.2.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332628 1113 #uuid "5fa96fb7-112b-583a-9e7b-b81a74ec73ed" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332628 1145 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-2)^{′}=3{x}^{2}+6x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{3}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-2}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+6{x}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(3{x}_{n}^{2}+6{x}_{n})-({x}_{n}^{3}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-2)}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+6{x}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}_{n}^{3}+3{x}_{n}^{2}+2}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+6{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=-1.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{2{x}_{0}^{3}+3{x}_{0}^{2}+2}{3{x}_{0}^{2}+6{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-1.2)^{3}+3(-1.2)^{2}+2}{3(-1.2)^{2}+6(-1.2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2.864}{(-2.88)} \\\\  & ≈-0.994,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535128 true] [17592186332629 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332630 13194139535128 true] [17592186332629 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535128 true] [17592186332629 1041 "field-1" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332629 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332630 13194139535128 true] [17592186332629 1110 #uuid "eb09ae4b-1103-5001-8859-e18004577fac" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332630 1034 "-0.994" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332630 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535128 true] [17592186332630 1111 #uuid "186aa665-bf9e-5147-a54e-b9f6182e39c1" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332631 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332632 13194139535128 true] [17592186332631 1089 "q-256484" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332631 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535128 true] [17592186332631 1100 "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={e}^{2x}+3{x}^{2}-2$ in the interval $[0,1].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=0.5.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332631 1113 #uuid "167af0d8-e301-5a6e-b854-9dc72bb28955" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332631 1145 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({e}^{2x}+3{x}^{2})^{′}=2{e}^{2x}+6x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{2{x}_{n}}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-2}{2{e}^{2{x}_{n}}+6{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{e}^{2{x}_{0}}+3{x}_{0}^{2}-2}{2{e}^{2{x}_{0}}+6{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =0.5-\\frac{{e}^{2\\cdot 0.5}+3\\cdot {0.5}^{2}-2}{2{e}^{2\\cdot 0.5}+6\\cdot 0.5} \\\\  & ≈0.326,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535128 true] [17592186332632 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332633 13194139535128 true] [17592186332632 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535128 true] [17592186332632 1041 "field-1" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332632 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332633 13194139535128 true] [17592186332632 1110 #uuid "6e4789c2-8175-5f03-91de-262539ba195a" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332633 1034 "0.326" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332633 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535128 true] [17592186332633 1111 #uuid "a26d3869-aa87-5469-8fc2-db4be27ec48c" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332634 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332635 13194139535128 true] [17592186332634 1089 "q-256498" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332634 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535128 true] [17592186332634 1100 "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{x}{10}$ in the interval $[6,8].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=7.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332634 1113 #uuid "eeef1a77-6847-55fa-ad0a-a25742783b5e" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332634 1145 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{x}{10})}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}}{10}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}_{n}-\\frac{1}{10}} \\\\  & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{10\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{n}-{x}_{n}}{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}_{n}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{10\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{0}-{x}_{0}}{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}_{0}-1} \\\\  & =7-\\frac{10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7)-7}{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7)-1} \\\\  & ≈7.066,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535128 true] [17592186332635 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332636 13194139535128 true] [17592186332635 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535128 true] [17592186332635 1041 "field-1" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332635 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332636 13194139535128 true] [17592186332635 1110 #uuid "25b8cc8e-ee03-52c6-b79c-362bfe04e0df" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332636 1034 "7.066" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332636 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535128 true] [17592186332636 1111 #uuid "4a2493cd-9475-503c-b8d4-2f931679b07e" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332637 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332638 13194139535128 true] [17592186332637 1089 "q-256503" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332637 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535128 true] [17592186332637 1100 "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={e}^{x}-{x}^{2}-2$ in the interval $[1,2].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=1.4.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332637 1113 #uuid "acfaee54-31cf-5487-90a0-acb4fb2b9fa9" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332637 1145 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({e}^{x}-{x}^{2}-2)^{′}={e}^{x}-2x\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}_{n}^{x}-{x}_{n}^{2}-2}{{e}_{n}^{x}-2{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{e}_{0}^{x}-{x}_{0}^{2}-2}{{e}_{0}^{x}-2{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =1.4-\\frac{{e}^{1.4}-{1.4}^{2}-2}{{e}^{1.4}-2\\cdot 1.4} \\\\  & =1.4-0.075\\,844… \\\\  & =1.324\\,155….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{{e}_{1}^{x}-{x}_{1}^{2}-2}{{e}_{1}^{x}-2{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =(1.324\\,155…)-\\frac{{e}^{1.324\\,155…}-(1.324\\,155…)^{2}-2}{{e}^{1.324\\,155…}-2\\cdot (1.324\\,155…)} \\\\  & ≈1.319,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535128 true] [17592186332638 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332639 13194139535128 true] [17592186332638 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535128 true] [17592186332638 1041 "field-1" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332638 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332639 13194139535128 true] [17592186332638 1110 #uuid "8758a2fa-233e-5e75-9277-b7316e2b3dec" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332639 1034 "1.319" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332639 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535128 true] [17592186332639 1111 #uuid "a671634a-e152-5766-a350-c8d8e6c552f5" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332640 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332641 13194139535128 true] [17592186332640 1089 "q-256510" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332640 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535128 true] [17592186332640 1100 "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-2x-1$ in the interval $[1,2].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=1.5.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332640 1113 #uuid "06f30e47-4074-56c3-99fb-97fdf1cb5bac" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332640 1145 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{4}-2x-1)^{′}=4{x}^{3}-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}^{4}-2x-1}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(4{x}_{n}^{3}-2)-({x}_{n}^{4}-2{x}_{n}-1)}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}_{n}^{4}+1}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{3{x}_{0}^{4}+1}{4{x}_{0}^{3}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1.5)^{4}+1}{4(1.5)^{3}-2} \\\\  & =1.407\\,608…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{3{x}_{1}^{4}-1}{4{x}_{1}^{3}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1.407\\,608…)^{4}-1}{4(1.407\\,608…)^{3}-2} \\\\  & ≈1.396,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535128 true] [17592186332641 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332642 13194139535128 true] [17592186332641 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535128 true] [17592186332641 1041 "field-1" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332641 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332642 13194139535128 true] [17592186332641 1110 #uuid "d99b0d46-9bf7-53b8-830b-9b8bbc3d3886" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332642 1034 "1.396" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332642 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535128 true] [17592186332642 1111 #uuid "b0cd3235-6427-5927-a518-24d211147824" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332643 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332644 13194139535128 true] [17592186332643 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332646 13194139535128 true] [17592186332643 1089 "q-256554" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332643 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535128 true] [17592186332643 1100 "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})-x+1$ in the interval $[1.8,2.2].$ Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=1.8.$ Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,{x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332643 1113 #uuid "9b889581-1a6e-55a5-bd9e-7cbc19420698" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332643 1145 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})-x+1)^{′}=2x{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}_{n}^{2})-{x}_{n}+1}{2{x}_{n}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}_{n}^{2})-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}_{0}^{2})-{x}_{0}+1}{2{x}_{0}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}_{0}^{2})-1} \\\\  & =1.8-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({1.8}^{2})-1.8+1}{2(1.8){sec}^{2}⁡({1.8}^{2})-1} \\\\  & =2.066\\,129…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}_{1}^{2})-{x}_{1}+1}{2{x}_{1}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}_{1}^{2})-1} \\\\  & =(2.066\\,129…)-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡((2.066\\,129…)^{2})-(2.066\\,129…)+1}{2(2.066\\,129…){sec}^{2}⁡((2.066\\,129…)^{2})-1} \\\\  & =2.017\\,679….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}≈2.066$ and ${x}_{2}≈2.018,$ rounded to three decimal places." 13194139535128 true] [17592186332644 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332645 13194139535128 true] [17592186332644 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535128 true] [17592186332644 1041 "field-1" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332644 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332645 13194139535128 true] [17592186332644 1110 #uuid "8250e28d-0fbc-550b-82b7-f6e00612d89f" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332645 1034 "2.066" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332645 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535128 true] [17592186332645 1111 #uuid "44356250-45a6-53f9-8aa0-e4614fe7b7fb" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332646 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332647 13194139535128 true] [17592186332646 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535128 true] [17592186332646 1041 "field-2" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332646 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332647 13194139535128 true] [17592186332646 1110 #uuid "d9fedcf7-700e-5ca0-9944-a5fa80045ba8" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332647 1034 "2.018" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332647 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535128 true] [17592186332647 1111 #uuid "c0d075df-5acf-554e-89f3-b470da23230f" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332648 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332649 13194139535128 true] [17592186332648 1089 "q-256582" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332648 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535128 true] [17592186332648 1100 "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+4x-6$ has a root in the interval $[0,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process starting from ${x}_{0}=1.3$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332648 1113 #uuid "e6070c72-350d-537f-91a2-dd7ce5ee69e6" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332648 1145 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{3}+4x-6)^{′}=3{x}^{2}+4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{3}+4{x}_{n}-6}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(3{x}_{n}^{2}+4)-({x}_{n}^{3}+4{x}_{n}-6)}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}_{n}^{3}+6}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{2{x}_{0}^{3}+6}{3{x}_{0}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.3)^{3}+6}{3(1.3)^{2}+4} \\\\  & =1.145…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{2{x}_{1}^{3}+6}{3{x}_{1}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.145…)^{3}+6}{3(1.145…)^{2}+4} \\\\  & =1.134…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{2{x}_{2}^{3}+6}{3{x}_{2}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.134…)^{3}+6}{3(1.134…)^{2}+4} \\\\  & =1.134….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}≈1.13$ and ${x}_{3}≈1.13$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x≈1.13,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139535128 true] [17592186332649 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332650 13194139535128 true] [17592186332649 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535128 true] [17592186332649 1041 "field-1" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332649 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332650 13194139535128 true] [17592186332649 1110 #uuid "bec568ec-0ddb-5c17-a5a7-3126d74f7d11" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332650 1034 "1.13" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332650 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535128 true] [17592186332650 1111 #uuid "8c2bc3da-491b-57d6-87df-ff4fdbdaf79c" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332651 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332652 13194139535128 true] [17592186332651 1089 "q-256588" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332651 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535128 true] [17592186332651 1100 "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-x-1$ has a root in the interval $[0,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process starting from ${x}_{0}=1.5$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332651 1113 #uuid "46f3d11c-933c-5f1f-bb46-5ce326bd0899" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332651 1145 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{3}-x-1)^{′}=3{x}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{3}-{x}_{n}-1}{3{x}_{n}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(3{x}_{n}^{2}-1)-({x}_{n}^{3}-{x}_{n}-1)}{3{x}_{n}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}_{n}^{3}+1}{3{x}_{n}^{2}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{2{x}_{0}^{3}+1}{3{x}_{0}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.5)^{3}+1}{3(1.5)^{2}-1} \\\\  & =1.347….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{2{x}_{1}^{3}+1}{3{x}_{1}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.347…)^{3}+1}{3(1.347…)^{2}-1} \\\\  & =1.325…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{2{x}_{2}^{3}+1}{3{x}_{2}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.325…)^{3}+1}{3(1.325…)^{2}-1} \\\\  & =1.324…, \\\\ {x}_{4} & =\\frac{2{x}_{3}^{3}+1}{3{x}_{3}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.324…)^{3}+1}{3(1.324…)^{2}-1} \\\\  & =1.324….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{3}≈1.32$ and ${x}_{4}≈1.32$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x≈1.32,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139535128 true] [17592186332652 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332653 13194139535128 true] [17592186332652 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535128 true] [17592186332652 1041 "field-1" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332652 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332653 13194139535128 true] [17592186332652 1110 #uuid "44d28fde-99f5-5e6f-a5cc-06f6478dfade" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332653 1034 "1.32" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332653 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535128 true] [17592186332653 1111 #uuid "0981a8d9-35d6-536e-9bf8-913c773c6fc5" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332654 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332655 13194139535128 true] [17592186332654 1089 "q-256612" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332654 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535128 true] [17592186332654 1100 "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-8x-3$ has a root in the interval $[1,3],$ use the Newton-Raphson process starting from ${x}_{0}=2$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332654 1113 #uuid "f3a35506-9e8d-588e-a12b-00a24cb42738" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332654 1145 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{4}-8x-3)^{′}=4{x}^{3}-8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{4}-8{x}_{n}-3}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-8} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(4{x}_{n}^{3}-8)-({x}_{n}^{4}-8{x}_{n}-3)}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({x}_{n}^{4}+1)}{4({x}_{n}^{3}-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{3({x}_{0}^{4}+1)}{4({x}_{0}^{3}-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({2}^{4}+1)}{4({2}^{3}-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{24} \\\\  & =2.125.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{3({x}_{1}^{4}+1)}{4({x}_{1}^{3}-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3((2.125)^{4}+1)}{4((2.125)^{3}-2)} \\\\  & =2.112…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{3({x}_{2}^{4}+1)}{4({x}_{2}^{3}-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3((2.112…)^{4}+1)}{4((2.112…)^{3}-2)} \\\\  & =2.111….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}≈2.11$ and ${x}_{3}≈2.11$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x≈2.11,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139535128 true] [17592186332655 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332656 13194139535128 true] [17592186332655 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535128 true] [17592186332655 1041 "field-1" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332655 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332656 13194139535128 true] [17592186332655 1110 #uuid "e0d035eb-70cb-5dd2-bddc-d57235945032" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332656 1034 "2.11" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332656 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535128 true] [17592186332656 1111 #uuid "01bb4bb2-f929-5ab0-9dd8-bc55fb561204" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332657 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332658 13194139535128 true] [17592186332657 1089 "q-256622" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332657 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535128 true] [17592186332657 1100 "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={e}^{x}+{x}^{3}-5x$ in the interval $[1,2].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=1.5.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332657 1113 #uuid "398d1164-07b2-5d0e-8a7a-6343a75009a5" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332657 1145 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({e}^{x}+{x}^{3}-5x)^{′}={e}^{x}+3{x}^{2}-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}_{n}^{x}+{x}_{n}^{3}-5{x}_{n}}{{e}_{n}^{x}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}({e}_{n}^{x}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-5)-({e}_{n}^{x}+{x}_{n}^{3}-5{x}_{n})}{{e}_{n}^{x}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}{e}^{{x}_{n}}+2{x}_{n}^{3}-{e}^{{x}_{n}}}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{{x}_{0}{e}^{{x}_{0}}+2{x}_{0}^{3}-{e}^{{x}_{0}}}{{e}^{{x}_{0}}+3{x}_{0}^{2}-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1.5){e}^{1.5}+2(1.5)^{3}-{e}^{1.5}}{{e}^{1.5}+3(1.5)^{2}-5} \\\\  & =1.442\\,762….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{{x}_{1}{e}^{{x}_{1}}+2{x}_{1}^{3}-{e}^{{x}_{1}}}{{e}^{{x}_{1}}+3{x}_{1}^{2}-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1.442\\,762…){e}^{1.442\\,762…}+2(1.442\\,762…)^{3}-{e}^{1.442\\,762…}}{{e}^{1.442\\,762…}+3(1.442\\,762…)^{2}-5} \\\\  & ≈1.439,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535128 true] [17592186332658 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332659 13194139535128 true] [17592186332658 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332660 13194139535128 true] [17592186332658 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535128 true] [17592186332658 1041 "field-1" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332658 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332659 13194139535128 true] [17592186332658 1110 #uuid "68bfca7d-b85d-554b-82d8-4ca24ff2ae00" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332659 1034 "1.439" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332659 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535128 true] [17592186332659 1111 #uuid "4baf06cd-06e4-5bab-9677-0f2a3e231967" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332660 1034 "1.443" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332660 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535128 true] [17592186332660 1111 #uuid "fcaa9737-27f8-5149-bbcb-cc33d694d67a" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332661 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332662 13194139535128 true] [17592186332661 1089 "q-256623" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332661 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535128 true] [17592186332661 1100 "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={e}^{-x}+{x}^{3}-2x$ in the interval $[0,1].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=0.5.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332661 1113 #uuid "f210ebf4-a8d3-532f-b4c7-22a04d1c64b7" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332661 1145 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({e}^{-x}+{x}^{3}-2x)^{′}=-{e}^{-x}+3{x}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{-{x}_{n}}+{x}_{n}^{3}-2{x}_{n}}{-{e}^{-{x}_{n}}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{e}^{-{x}_{0}}+{x}_{0}^{3}-2{x}_{0}}{-{e}^{-{x}_{0}}+3{x}_{0}^{2}-2} \\\\  & =0.5-\\frac{{e}^{-0.5}+{0.5}^{3}-2\\cdot 0.5}{-{e}^{-0.5}+3\\cdot {0.5}^{2}-2} \\\\  & ≈0.355,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535128 true] [17592186332662 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332663 13194139535128 true] [17592186332662 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332664 13194139535128 true] [17592186332662 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535128 true] [17592186332662 1041 "field-1" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332662 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332664 13194139535128 true] [17592186332662 1110 #uuid "8edea445-664a-52a1-9f48-4facfb64bf5b" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332663 1034 "0.645" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332663 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535128 true] [17592186332663 1111 #uuid "13991cdc-597d-5077-ac18-00b4667fcb96" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332664 1034 "0.355" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332664 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535128 true] [17592186332664 1111 #uuid "e0c503ee-cfe0-554a-bd28-75c96f11eb20" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332665 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332666 13194139535128 true] [17592186332665 1089 "q-339531" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332665 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535128 true] [17592186332665 1100 "Given that the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-{e}^{x}$  has a root in the interval $[1,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process starting from ${x}_{0}=1$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332665 1113 #uuid "d52f717c-4da1-5117-bc70-35aea56a2d70" 13194139535128 true] [17592186332665 1145 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(2{x}^{3}-{e}^{x})^{′}=6{x}^{2}-{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{2{x}_{n}^{3}-{e}^{{x}_{n}}}{6{x}_{n}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{n}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(6{x}_{n}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{n}})-(2{x}_{n}^{3}-{e}^{{x}_{n}})}{6{x}_{n}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{n}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}_{n}^{3}-{x}_{n}{e}^{{x}_{n}}+{e}^{{x}_{n}}}{6{x}_{n}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{n}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{4{x}_{0}^{3}-{x}_{0}{e}^{{x}_{0}}+{e}^{{x}_{0}}}{6{x}_{0}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{0}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1)^{3}-(1){e}^{1}+{e}^{1}}{6(1)^{2}-{e}^{1}} \\\\  & =1.218\\,873….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{4{x}_{1}^{3}-{x}_{1}{e}^{{x}_{1}}+{e}^{{x}_{1}}}{6{x}_{1}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{1}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1.218\\,873…)^{3}-(1.218\\,873…){e}^{1.218\\,873…}+{e}^{1.218\\,873…}}{6(1.218\\,873…)^{2}-{e}^{1.218\\,873…}} \\\\  & =1.175\\,790…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{4{x}_{2}^{3}-{x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{2}}+{e}^{{x}_{2}}}{6{x}_{2}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1.175\\,790…)^{3}-(1.175\\,790…){e}^{1.175\\,790…}+{e}^{1.175\\,790…}}{6(1.175\\,790…)^{2}-{e}^{1.175\\,790…}} \\\\  & =1.173\\,749…, \\\\ {x}_{4} & =\\frac{4{x}_{3}^{3}-{x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{3}}+{e}^{{x}_{3}}}{6{x}_{3}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1.173\\,749…)^{3}-(1.173\\,749…){e}^{1.173\\,749…}+{e}^{1.173\\,749…}}{6(1.173\\,749…)^{2}-{e}^{1.173\\,749…}} \\\\  & =1.173\\,744…,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{3}≈1.174$ and ${x}_{4}≈1.174$ are the same when rounded to three decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x≈1.174,$ rounded to three decimal places." 13194139535128 true] [17592186332666 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332667 13194139535128 true] [17592186332666 1000 #edb/ref 